Description: Given a matrix of the strength of relation of members of two
    sets (columns and rows), for each row it tests whether there are column(s)
    for which the row is significantly more relevant than for the other
    column(s). The row is referred to as a marker, and the column(s) are
    friend(s) of the marker. Thus a specific association is identified rather
    than just the strongest one. The potential applications are numerous, from
    refining fuzzy clustering to specific gene regulation. The columns are
    ranked independently of one another, so they do not need to share a scale,
    and matrices assembled from different sources can be analysed.
